WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.378 --> 00:00:03.211
(energetic music)

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.020
- [R. Stevie Moore] Well,
I'm R. Stevie Moore.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:11.110
I'm generally known as
one of the main pioneers

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:15.123
of low fidelity one-man
band home recording.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.950
I've been recording
for 50-something years,

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:22.513
and I've made 400 albums in my home.

00:00:22.513 --> 00:00:24.972
♪ I don't think about ♪

00:00:24.972 --> 00:00:29.394
♪ About appearing anywhere ♪

00:00:29.394 --> 00:00:33.760
♪ I got enough to do right here ♪

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.240
- [Radio Voice] And now
more R. Stevie Moore.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.290
- [Narrator] R. Stevie Moore, prolific.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.650
- [Announcer] Mr. R. Stevie Moore.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.210
- R. Stevie Moore.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.630
- Yes, I am R. Stevie Moore.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.130
Back when I was a younger child, I started

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:49.150
my own idea of home recording by myself.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:52.590
My father was a bass player
doing all the country sessions

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.050
with all the stars of Nashville.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:59.370
I had access to tape recorders
in my teenage basement,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:03.860
and I was a huge rock
and roll fan and absorbed

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.010
all of these influences
and started messing around

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:09.956
with writing songs and
making my own music.

00:01:09.956 --> 00:01:11.940
(upbeat music)

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.340
- [Man] You record in the privacy

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:14.490
of your own home, is that correct?

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:15.323
- [R. Stevie Moore] Mmhm, that's right.

00:01:15.323 --> 00:01:16.156
- [Man] Now how do you do that?

00:01:16.156 --> 00:01:19.690
- Well, late 60s is when I
started making my little albums.

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.850
I overdub all the instruments
myself, I play bass

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:27.390
and guitars and keyboards
and percussion and vocals.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.270
I got really good at it.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.930
I can do seven, eight, nine, 10 overdubs

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:35.200
and you'd still hear the
very first thing that I use.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.450
Did you invent home recording?

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.380
Of course not, but I took it to another

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.913
level long before it was customary.

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.580
I've probably written
about 4 or 5,000 songs.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.760
They came out effortlessly.
I would compile

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.840
the most recent songs. I
wouldn't pick and choose

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.430
the best ones and throw the
worst ones on the floor.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:57.490
And that's been my aesthetic all along.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.430
It's like everything is
released, warts and all.

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:04.440
'Cause bad music is brilliant—
it's a diary of sound.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.290
Everybody that has ever
been a record collector

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.880
will have to turn their
pallets on to the obscure.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.270
Wait 'till you hear this one, I mean,

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.270
I do have that reputation.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:17.370
Talking Heads, and B-52s,
they were all fans of mine.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:20.220
Naturally, a lot of these big
artists have found my music

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.000
just word of mouth, but they never

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.390
saw me because I was the home hermit.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.820
I know what I'm doin', I keep tryin'

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.710
I keep doin' more of it,
I build up the quantity

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.420
without thinking about starting over

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.950
at the beginning to improve the quality.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.910
I hardly ever preformed,
and I was a bedroom guy

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:42.900
it was always get home,
put the headphones on,

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.343
and then I would work for hours and hours.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.560
In 2010, things started to explode.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.260
Out of nowhere, suddenly I was

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:54.040
in a band and touring the world.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.410
I was playing hard rock
shows, big festivals

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.180
and tiny dives. It was incredible.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.920
My career really started taking

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.608
off a little too late in life.

00:03:04.608 --> 00:03:07.358
(crowd cheering)

00:03:08.774 --> 00:03:11.250
(guitar tuning)

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.237
Often people say, "I can't
see how you've done this same

00:03:14.237 --> 00:03:18.233
"thing all this time, without
falling through the trap."

00:03:18.233 --> 00:03:19.250
(gentle guitar music)

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:22.260
And by avoiding the
mainstream, nobody's gonna

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.080
dare tell me what I can and can't do.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:28.423
Feels so good to have
that integrity in tact.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.088
I'm proud of all that, and
I'm also still a little kid.

00:03:33.088 --> 00:03:36.255
(gentle guitar music)

